\section{Heap shape and the allocation contract}

A precise collector needs to know which words are references. One part of that information describes heap objects: their size, alignment, and reference-bearing fields. Andrew W.\ Appel's \emph{A Runtime System} describes how the Standard ML of New Jersey implementation derives substantial functionality from a simple representation based on pointers, records, and strings. A modern layout might use a type descriptor for a fixed-size object and a length plus an element kind for an array. The allocator and scanners must agree on how to recover object boundaries, including padding and any representation used after forwarding. Storing a pointer-looking integer in a scalar field must not turn that field into a root.

Fast allocation often bumps a pointer in a thread-local region, with an out-of-space path that can collect or refill the region. Before a new object becomes visible to a scanner, its header and every pointer slot the scanner may visit must contain valid values. This may mean leaving the object outside the published allocation range until initialized or initializing the fields to null before exposing it. A concurrently scanned heap also needs the corresponding publication ordering. The slow path is a call with a collector contract; references needed after it cannot be left in unreported scratch registers.

\section{Safepoints and precise frame maps}

Frame metadata answers a different question: where are the references at this stopped program counter? Interpreter frames may expose tagged values; compiled frames may use registers, spills, and caller-save locations whose original contents have been stored elsewhere. A safepoint map must pair with the code version and the stack-walking convention. The caller's map may be keyed by the return address, even though the currently executing callee has stopped elsewhere. A relocating collector must be able to rewrite each reported root that survives the stop. If a live machine register contains an interior address, either report how it derives from an identifiable base or reconstruct it from a base kept live across the stop.

For example, at the call to an allocation slow path, suppose a live object reference is spilled to slot 2 and a live integer occupies slot 3. If collection moves the object from address $A$ to $B$, slot 2 is rewritten to $B$, and slot 3 remains unchanged even if its bits equal $A$. After the call, a reload must use slot 2, not a copy of $A$ in an unreported register. A small test VM can force a move at every slow allocation and check a later read through that reference. Running the same test with and without the spill optimization catches missing maps. Keep deoptimization recipes separate: Chapter~04 of the Modern Compiler Dossier, \emph{Deoptimization as an IR Operation}, goes beyond root locations to describe reconstructed frames and materialized objects.

\section{Recording heap mutations}

An old-to-young reference can be the only way a nursery object is reachable. Without a barrier, a collector that scans roots and the nursery but skips old objects may reclaim it. A common contract has reference stores mark the card containing an old destination that may now point into the nursery; the minor collector scans remembered cards as well as roots. A card is a conservative filter over addresses, not a substitute for object layout metadata. David A.\ Moon's \emph{Garbage Collection in a Large Lisp System} describes how tracking references to ephemeral objects on the Symbolics 3600 sought to avoid scanning an enormous, demand-paged root set. The problem it illustrates is both one of graph reachability and one of memory locality.

Test by storing a fresh object into an old array, dropping the only direct root, and forcing a minor collection. The array element must still identify the object and must be updated if it moved. Repeat through each store path, including bulk copying. Separately check that remembered information is not cleared while an unrecorded old-to-young edge remains. A concurrent marking collector can require a different barrier, such as logging overwritten references; a new-value generational barrier does not provide that guarantee by itself.

\section{Roots outside managed frames}

Globals, code objects, and foreign handles can hold roots too. Marcus Crestani's \emph{Foreign-Function Interfaces for Garbage-Collected Programming Languages} describes explicit root registration and reference objects as ways to track values that native code might otherwise hide. Give each foreign root an owner and a well-defined last use. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-250} discusses transferring those lifetimes across a callback; Chapter~\ref{ch:garbage-collection} deals with the collector algorithms themselves.
